TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-02739124
TI  - Varenicline and Nicotine Replacement Therapy for Smokers Admitted to Hospitals: A Randomized Clinical Trial
AU  - Weeks, G. R.
AU  - Gobarani, R. K.
AU  - George, J.
AB  - IMPORTANCE: Varenicline is the most effective sole pharmacotherapy for smoking cessation. If used in combination with nicotine replacement therapy (NRT), cessation rates may be further improved, but the efficacy and safety of the combination need to be evaluated. INTERVENTIONS: A 12-week varenicline regimen was initiated during hospitalization at standard doses in all participants. Participants were randomized to additionally use NRT (2 mg) or placebo lozenges if there was an urge to smoke.
PY  - 2024
DO  - 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.18120
JO  - JAMA Network Open
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-NRT-001
TI  - Nicotine patch versus placebo patch for smoking cessation in adult smokers: a randomised controlled trial
AU  - Hartmann, Lena
AU  - Okafor, Daniel
AB  - BACKGROUND: Transdermal nicotine is widely used, but trials in primary care remain few. METHODS: In this double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled trial, 412 adults aged 18 years or older who smoked at least 10 cigarettes per day were assigned to a 16-hour nicotine patch or a placebo patch for 12 weeks, with brief behavioural support. The primary outcome was biochemically validated sustained abstinence at 6 months. RESULTS: Sustained abstinence at 6 months was 19.4% with the nicotine patch and 10.7% with placebo (risk ratio 1.81, 95% CI 1.12 to 2.93). CONCLUSIONS: The nicotine patch nearly doubled quit rates in adult smokers in primary care. Trial registration: NCT01234561.
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1000/nrt.2019.001
JO  - Addiction Research
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-NRT-002
TI  - Nicotine gum for smoking cessation in hospital inpatients: a randomized trial
AU  - Brennan, Siobhan
AB  - BACKGROUND: Admission to hospital is an opportunity to stop smoking. METHODS: We randomized 280 adult inpatients who smoked tobacco daily to 4 mg nicotine gum or no nicotine replacement, both with counselling. Follow-up was at 6 and 12 months. RESULTS: Validated abstinence at 12 months was 15.0% with nicotine gum and 8.6% without (risk ratio 1.75, 95% CI 1.01 to 3.03). CONCLUSIONS: Nicotine gum started in hospital increased long-term abstinence.
PY  - 2017
DO  - 10.1000/nrt.2017.014
JO  - Journal of Hospital Medicine Trials
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-NRT-003
TI  - Combination nicotine replacement (patch plus lozenge) versus patch alone: a randomised controlled trial
AU  - Ivanova, Maria
AU  - Lund, Erik
AB  - METHODS: This open-label randomised controlled trial enrolled 600 adult smokers who wanted to quit. Participants received a nicotine patch plus a 2 mg nicotine lozenge, or the nicotine patch alone, for 12 weeks. RESULTS: Abstinence at 26 weeks was 24.1% with combination therapy and 17.3% with the patch alone (risk ratio 1.39, 95% CI 1.02 to 1.90). CONCLUSIONS: Adding a fast-acting form of nicotine replacement to the patch improves quit rates.
PY  - 2021
DO  - 10.1000/nrt.2021.033
JO  - Tobacco Control Trials
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-NRT-004
TI  - Transdermal nicotine for active ulcerative colitis: a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial
AU  - Pereira, Joana
AB  - METHODS: We enrolled 64 adults with active ulcerative colitis who did not smoke tobacco. Patients were randomly assigned to transdermal nicotine patches or placebo patches for six weeks. RESULTS: Clinical remission occurred in 12 of 31 patients with nicotine and 6 of 33 with placebo. CONCLUSIONS: Transdermal nicotine may induce remission in ulcerative colitis.
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1000/gut.2016.207
JO  - Inflammatory Bowel Trials
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-NRT-005
TI  - Varenicline versus placebo for smoking cessation in adults with depression: a randomized trial
AU  - Novak, Tomas
AB  - METHODS: In this randomized, double-blind trial, 525 adult smokers with stable depression received varenicline 1 mg twice daily or placebo for 12 weeks. RESULTS: Continuous abstinence from week 9 to week 52 was 20.3% with varenicline and 10.4% with placebo. CONCLUSIONS: Varenicline helped adults with depression stop smoking.
PY  - 2018
DO  - 10.1000/dep.2018.551
JO  - Psychiatry Trials
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
ID  - CN-NRT-006
TI  - Use of nicotine replacement therapy and quit success in the general population: a prospective cohort study
AU  - Clarke, Hannah
AB  - METHODS: We followed 3,214 adult smokers who made a quit attempt, recording whether they used nicotine replacement therapy bought over the counter. RESULTS: After adjustment, use of over-the-counter nicotine replacement was not associated with abstinence at 6 months (odds ratio 1.04, 95% CI 0.85 to 1.27). CONCLUSIONS: Unsupervised use of nicotine replacement shows no benefit in routine settings.
PY  - 2020
DO  - 10.1000/pop.2020.090
